23:28:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great observation, and the answer comes down to the massive difference between **industrial printing** and **home printing**, both in technology and economics. ### 1. The ink is a completely different beast At home, you’re using an inkjet or laser printer with tiny cartridges. These cartridges are designed for convenience, not cost-efficiency. The “ink” you buy is actually a complex, precisely engineered fluid packaged in a cartridge with microchip intelligence—all sold at a huge markup (the classic razor-and-blades business model). You might pay $20–$30 for a cartridge that contains only a few millilitres of ink, making the *per-millilitre* cost astronomically high. Newspapers are printed on giant web-offset presses using **offset ink** (a thick, paste-like substance, not the watery stuff from cartridges). They buy ink by the barrel or tote—literally thousands of litres at a time. On an industrial scale, offset ink can cost as little as **$3–$5 per kilogram**. A few grams of ink can print hundreds of pages, so the ink cost per page is a fraction of a cent. ### 2. Economies of scale - **Paper:** Newsprint is bought in enormous rolls that weigh several tons each. By ordering thousands of tons annually, a newspaper publisher negotiates rock-bottom prices—often pennies per sheet. If you buy a ream of paper at an office supply store, you’re paying many times more per sheet just for packaging, retail markup, and low volume. - **Printing equipment:** A modern newspaper press runs at speeds up to 80,000 copies per hour, printing both sides in full colour in a single pass. The fixed costs of the press, building, and maintenance are spread over millions of copies daily. When you print one copy at home, you’re bearing the full amortised cost of your printer, plus the expensive ink cartridges—no volume spreading. ### 3. Advertising pays most of the bill The cover price of a newspaper often doesn’t even cover the raw materials and printing. The real revenue comes from **advertising**. Ads can account for 70–80% of a newspaper’s income. The price you pay at the newsstand is heavily subsidised by advertisers, making it artificially low compared to what it actually costs to produce. If you had to pay the full unsubsidised cost as a consumer printing a single copy, it would be far more expensive. ### 4. The efficiency of offset printing Offset presses use a process where ink is transferred from a plate to a rubber blanket, then to the paper. This uses a **very thin film of ink**—much less than what an inkjet squirts onto a page. Combined with fast-drying cold-set inks (which soak into the porous newsprint), waste is minimised. Home printers often lay down more ink to achieve vibrant colour on coated paper, and a lot gets wasted during cleaning cycles. ### 5. Putting it in numbers (rough illustration) - Newspaper ink cost per page: ~0.1¢ or less - Newsprint cost per double-page: ~1–2¢ - Total materials for a 40-page colour newspaper: maybe 20–40¢ - Cover price: $1–$2 (but the reader only pays that; advertisers cover the rest) Meanwhile, printing one full-colour page at home can easily use $0.50–$1.00 worth of ink alone—because your cartridge is mostly marketing, packaging, and R&D cost amortised over a tiny volume. So you’re absolutely right: if you tried to replicate a single newspaper on a home printer using consumer ink, it would cost 20 times the newsstand price. But once you scale up to industrial machinery, buy raw materials by the ton, and let advertisers foot most of the bill, that dirt-cheap price suddenly makes sense.
