23:28:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Ah, I see what you’re getting at now—thank you for clarifying! You’re not just asking about ink cost in general; you’re saying, “Given how vibrant and colourful the pictures are, surely that must use a *ton* of expensive ink, so how can it possibly be cheap?” That’s a completely fair assumption. Let me clarify exactly why even full-colour, photo-quality newspaper pages are astonishingly cheap to print at industrial scale. ### The secret is not expensive ink; it’s an optical illusion using dots A colour photograph in a newspaper might look like a continuous, rich spectrum of colours, but it’s actually made entirely of tiny dots of just **four ink colours**: Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, and Black (CMYK). This is called **halftone printing**. - The image is broken down into a grid of microscopic dots. - The dots vary in size and spacing to trick your eye into seeing continuous tones. A bright red area is just a dense pattern of magenta and yellow dots; a sky blue is cyan and a few magenta dots; dark brown is all four. - The actual **ink coverage** (the percentage of the paper surface covered by ink) is surprisingly low. A vibrant photo might use only 20–30% ink coverage on average — even less on porous newsprint. So when you see a vivid color image, you’re not looking at thick layers of expensive pigment. You’re looking at a sparse pattern of tiny dots using the same cheap bulk ink that the text uses, just arranged cleverly. The paper itself provides the white, and the thin ink film does the colour mixing. ### The ink itself is dirt cheap As before: newspaper printers use offset **paste inks** bought by the ton for a few dollars per kilogram. A single kilogram can print hundreds of thousands of pages. The pigments (even for cyan, magenta, yellow) are commodity chemicals at that scale — nothing boutique. The cost of ink per full-colour page works out to around **0.1–0.2 cents**. That’s two-thousandths of a dollar, not twenty. ### The process is optimized for minimal ink use Offset presses transfer ink from a roller to a plate to a rubber blanket to the paper in an extremely thin, even film. The newsprint itself is designed to absorb the ink just enough to dry instantly (cold-set) without spreading. There’s no waste from cleaning cycles splurging ink down the drain (which home inkjets do). And the press runs so fast that any fixed setup cost (plates, makeready) is negligible per copy. ### Why your home print looks expensive by comparison Even if you print a full-colour photo on your inkjet, you’re laying down tiny droplets of ink that are much thicker and less efficient. Consumer ink is a retail product with enormous margins, and inkjet cartridges notoriously waste a lot of ink on maintenance. But even more importantly, the ink cost per page for a home printer is hundreds of times higher per millilitre because you’re buying a tiny cartridge at a retail price point, not a tanker of raw offset ink. ### So to answer your revised point directly: **Vibrant colour does not equal expensive ink in newspaper printing.** It’s a triumph of optical engineering — using just four cheap colours, printed in tiny dots, on cheap paper, at blinding speed, with advertising covering the bill. The image you see looks lush because your eye blends the dots, not because the newspaper poured exotic pigments onto the page. If you actually looked at a newspaper photo under a magnifying glass, you’d see a sparse dot pattern that uses hardly any material at all. That’s how they can sell you that colourful paper for pocket change. Does that clear it up?
