uld remain tiny Suppose a newspaper switched to a slightly more expensive ink formulation for better rub-resistance or slightly purer hue. At $6/kg instead of $4/kg, the ink cost per page might rise from 0.1 cents to 0.15 cents — still nothing. Ink cost per page is dominated by the *amount* used and the scale of purchase, not the pigment grade. The bottleneck to cost is not the quality; it’s the retail packaging and distribution of consumer ink. ### 4. An analogy: house paint vs. artist’s paint Think of it like this: if you buy a tiny tube of professional watercolour paint, it’s very expensive per millilitre because it’s high pigment load, high purity, and sold in tiny, fancy packages. But if a professional house painter buys exterior paint by the 20-litre bucket, even a very high-grade pigment (like a titanium white exterior acrylic) becomes cheap per square metre. Newspaper ink is the house painter’s bucket, not the artist’s tube — even if it has to hit a certain colour standard. ### 5. What really limits colour is the paper, not the ink The real reason newspaper colours can sometimes look a bit flat or muddy is the **newsprint itself** — it’s uncoated, absorbent, and often slightly grey. If newspapers wanted truly brilliant, magazine-quality colours, they’d have to use more expensive coated paper (like magazines do), which would drive up paper costs massively. They don’t. So they optimise for an acceptable colour gamut on the cheapest possible stock using the cheapest possible inks, yet the result still looks “colourful” because of the half-tone illusion. **Bottom line:** The colours pop not because of expensive, high-quality inks, but despite using the cheapest bulk inks available — all thanks to the physics of halftone dots on a white (ish) background. The cost factor you’re imagining simply isn’t there at industrial scale. So your assumption that “bright = expensive ink” is perfectly natural, but in this case, it’s the printing technique, not the ink quality, 